<hr>

<table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%">
<tr>
<td class="column-l">
<div class="fr1 mt1"><a href="./pdf/261025.pdf" target="news" onclick="gtag('config', 'UA-3981659-1', {'page_path': '/pdf/Race-result 2026/Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)'})">PDF(196kb)</a><img alt="" align="absmiddle" src="../_img/pdf05.gif" width="20" height="19" /></div>

<h5>Going to Sky Stands in Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger)</h5>


<div class="fr1 cb">
<!--
<a href="javascript:writeGallery('Oct. 25, 2026','Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/s2/261025a.jpg','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/b/261025a.jpg')"><img src="./images/261025/01.jpg" width="300" height="200" alt="Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)" /></a><br>
<br>
-->
<a href="javascript:writeGallery('Oct. 25, 2026','Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/s2/261025a.jpg','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/b/261025a.jpg')"><img src="./images/261025/02.jpg" width="300" height="200" alt="Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)" /></a><br>
<br>
<a href="javascript:writeGallery('Oct. 25, 2026','Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/s2/261025b.jpg','../en/racing/news_media/gallery/img/2026/b/261025b.jpg')"><img src="./images/261025/03.jpg" width="300" height="200" alt="Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)" /></a><br>
<br>
</div>



<p>Amid high expectations of a Triple Crown, third favorite <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001373080/" class="link01" target="_blank">Going to Sky</a></strong>, bouncing back from a sixth-place finish in the St. Lite Kinen (G2, 2,200m) on September 13, captured the final leg of the Triple Crown, the Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger), denying double-crown champion Lovcen the sweep. Sired by 2020 Triple Crown winner Contrail, the bay colt won his debut race in October (Tokyo, 2,000m) before registering a third in the Kyoto Nisai Stakes (G3, 2,000m) the next month. After finishing sixth in the Kisaragi Sho (G3, 1,800m) this February, he grabbed his first graded title in the Aoba Sho (G2, 2,400m) in April, which was followed by a fourth in the Tokyo Yushun (Japanese Derby, G1, 2,400m). This victory marked the first G1 title for both his sire and trainer Yuki Uehara who opened his yard in 2023 and had scored four grade-races prior to the Kikuka Sho. Jockey Yutaka Take won his 87th JRA-G1 title, his first since this year&rsquo;s Takarazuka Kinen aboard Meisho Tabaru, and sixth Kikuka Sho triumph after Super Creek (1988), Dance in the Dark (1996), Air Shakur (2000), Deep Impact (2005) and World Premiere (2019). He set a new record for the most Kikuka Sho victories.</p>
<p><strong>Going to Sky </strong>broke smoothly and took a ground-saving trip in mid-pack while <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001374036/" class="link01" target="_blank">King Glory</a></strong> gunned for the lead to set the pace, widely distancing himself with the rest of the field. The frontrunner&rsquo;s huge lead of nearly 20 lengths in the backstretch gradually dwindled as the field rounded the final corners. Entering the straight, the Contrail colt was shifted a few paths off the rail, advanced through an opening to take command 100 meters out and surged to the wire, narrowly holding off the runner-up&rsquo;s late charge to prevail by a nose.</p>
<p>&ldquo;I knew there were some strong horses in the field, but I wanted to focus on riding my own race. I&rsquo;ve ridden this colt several times now, and I&rsquo;d given a lot of thought to how best to ride him. He settled nicely in a good spot along the rail, and I felt we were in a very good rhythm. I decided to wait and make our move in the stretch. He responded well once given a green light and with about 200 meters to go, I thought we might win. He ran a good race in the Derby, and I felt we had a good chance in the last leg of the Triple Crown,&rdquo; commented Yutaka Take.</p>
<p>Eighth pick <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001369933/" class="link01" target="_blank">Basse Terre</a></strong> was unhurried from the start, traveling near the rear up to the final turn. Heading for home with the fastest last three-furlong speed, the Kitasan Black colt kicked into gear bumping a rival in early stretch, then squeezed through a gap while briefly blocking the race favorite and unleashed a strong drive to the wire to threaten the eventual winner for the runner-up spot.</p>
<p>Second choice <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001375373/" class="link01" target="_blank">Danon History</a></strong> traveled most of the distance wide around sixth and gradually improved position to enter the lane in third. Battling with rivals for the lead, the son of Epiphaneia was overtaken by the eventual winner on the inside and runner-up from the outside in the last 200 meters but found another gear in the final strides to secure third place while fending off the fast-closing Sanono Greater by half a length.</p>
<p>Heavily favored <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001374057/" class="link01" target="_blank">Lovcen</a></strong> broke poorly and was forced to sit among the trailing runners, fourth to fifth from the rear. Attempting to make up ground, the colt made rapid headway from the outside before the third corner and although hitting the stretch in good striking position, the star colt could not match the charge of his rivals and faded to 14th.</p>
<p><u>Other Horses:</u><br>
 4th: (12) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001371106/" class="link01" target="_blank">Sanono Greater</a></strong>—ran on rails around 12th, launched 2nd fastest late kick but belatedly<br>
 5th: (15) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001375213/" class="link01" target="_blank">Sea to Summit</a></strong>—chased distant leader in 2nd, ran gamely until 100m out<br>
 6th: (16) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001373525/" class="link01" target="_blank">Shonan Hayanami</a></strong>—traveled near rear, circled wide, showed belated charge<br>
 7th: (14) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001368295/" class="link01" target="_blank">Majorelle Bleu</a></strong>—settled around 8th, rallied for lead at 200m out, weakened in last 100m<br>
 8th: (2) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001374789/" class="link01" target="_blank">Congestus</a></strong>—traveled around 9th, showed effort but checked by rival at 200m pole<br>
 9th: (3) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001373599/" class="link01" target="_blank">Audacia</a></strong>—sat around 14th, advanced in backstretch, checked by rival at early stretch, showed little<br>
 10th: (5) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001377879/" class="link01" target="_blank">Justin Chicago</a></strong>—hugged rails in 6th early, dropped position in backstretch, showed brief effort<br>
 11th: (17) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001367572/" class="link01" target="_blank">Charing Cross</a></strong>—positioned wide around 9th, lacked needed kick<br>
 12th: (7) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001378293/" class="link01" target="_blank">Large Ensemble</a></strong>—raced around 5th, remained in contention until 200m pole<br>
 13th: (1) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001375501/" class="link01" target="_blank">M’s Begin</a></strong>—trailed in rear on rails, even paced at stretch<br>
 15th: (13) <strong>King Glory</strong>—set fast pace, extended lead to more than 20 lengths at one point, weakened<br>
 16th: (18) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001369318/" class="link01" target="_blank">Narita Esperanza</a></strong>—ran around 3rd, faded after 4th corner<br>
 17th: (11) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001372490/" class="link01" target="_blank">Ask Edinburgh</a></strong>—saved ground around 3rd, failed to sustain bid, dropped back<br>
 18th: (4) <strong><a href="https://www.jbis.jp/horse/0001373922/" class="link01" target="_blank">Shonan Gulf</a></strong>—traveled around 15th inside favorite, no factor</p>
<div class="fr1 c">
 <img src="./images/261025/racecourse.jpg" width="100" height="64" alt="">
</div>

<p><strong>THE 87TH KIKUKA SHO (JAPANESE ST. LEGER, G1)</strong><br>
 3-year-olds, Colts and Fillies, 3,000 meters (about 15 furlongs), turf, right-handed <br>
 Sunday, October 25, 2026          Kyoto Racecourse         11th Race         Post time: 15:40<strong></strong><br>
 Total prize money: &yen; 434,000,000 (about US$ 2,800,000 &lt;US$1=&yen;155&gt;)<br>
 3-y-o: 57 kg (about 126 lbs), 2 kg allowance for Fillies, 2 kg allowance for Southern Hemisphere-bred born in 2023<br>
 Course record: 3:01.0                Race record: 3:01.0 [Toho Jackal (JPN, by Special Week), 2014]<br>
 Safety factor: 18 runners            Going: Good to Firm      Weather: Cloudy</p>
<table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" class="t01">
  <tr>
 <th nowrap>FP</th>
 <th nowrap>BK</th>
 <th nowrap>PP</th>
 <th nowrap="nowrap">Horse<br>
 Jockey</th>
 <th nowrap>S&amp;A<br>
 Color<br>
 Wgt</th>
 <th nowrap="nowrap">Odds<br>
 (Fav)</th>
 <th nowrap="nowrap">Margin<br>
 (L3F)</th>
 <th nowrap="nowrap">Sire<br>
 Dam<br>
 (Dam&rsquo;s Sire)</th>
 <th nowrap="nowrap">Owner<br>
 Breeder<br>
 Trainer</th>
 </tr>
