<p class="p12">&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>* Kikuka Sho (Japanese St. Leger) (G1)</strong></p>
<p>The history of Kikuka Sho, the Japanese St. Leger and the last leg of the Triple Crown race for three-year-old colts and fillies, dates back to 1938—a year after Japan&rsquo;s 11 racing clubs integrated into one organization named Nihon Keiba Kai (forerunner of the JRA). Initially called &ldquo;Kyoto Norin-sho Shoten Yonsai Yobiuma Kyoso&rdquo;, the race was renamed to Kikuka Sho in 1948. The race was held at its temporary venue of Hanshin Racecourse in 2021 and 2022 while Kyoto Racecourse was undergoing renovation. <br>
The Kikuka Sho features three-year-olds, most of who have matured dramatically during the five months following the Tokyo Yushun (Japanese Derby, G1, 2,400m) and their summer break. The Kikuka Sho winners have both the speed and the stamina to prove their caliber as potential breeding stock. The last of the eight Triple Crown winners in JRA history was Contrail (JPN, by Deep Impact) who followed in the footsteps of his sire by accomplishing the feat undefeated in 2020.<br>
In the spotlight this year was <strong>Lovcen</strong>, the double crown champion one race away from the Triple Crown sweep. The World Premiere colt came into the race from a narrow win in the Kobe Shimbun Hai (G2, 2,400m, Sep.21) where <strong>Sea to Summit</strong> followed in a close third. Claiming the other trial race, the St. Lite Kinen (G2, 2,200m, Sep.13), was <strong>Justin Chicago</strong> with the Spring Stakes (G2, 1,800m) victor <strong>Audacia</strong> in third. The last leg of the Classics also included <strong>Going to Sky</strong>, <strong>Congestus</strong>, <strong>Sanono Greater</strong> and <strong>Danon History</strong>, respective winners of the Aoba Sho (G2, 2,400m), the Kyoto Shimbun Hai (G2, 2,200m), the Radio Nikkei Sho (G3, 1,800m) and the Akanko Tokubetsu (2 Wins Class, 2,600m, Aug.16), as well as <strong>Ask Edinburgh</strong> and <strong>Basse Terre</strong>, who finished fourth in the Satsuki Sho (Japanese 2000 Guineas, G1, 2,000m) and third in the Tokyo Yushun, respectively.</p></td>
